= a
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

For a bounded volume $V$ with piecewise smooth closed boundary $S=\partial V$, and a continuously differentiable <vector field> $\mathbf F$ on a neighbourhood of $\overline V$, the <divergence theorem> states
$$
\boxed{\iint_S\mathbf F\cdot\hat{\mathbf n}\,dS=\iiint_V\nabla\cdot\mathbf F\,dV.}
$$
Here $\hat{\mathbf n}$ is the outward <unit normal vector>. The left side is the outward <flux>; the right side integrates the <divergence> over the enclosed volume.

= b
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

The upper piece is a downward-opening circular <elliptic paraboloid> cap, running from the circle $r=1,z=1$ to the apex $r=0,z=2$. The middle piece is the side of a circular <cylinder> of radius one and height one. The lower piece is the unit <disk> in the horizontal plane. Their union is the closed boundary of
$$
V=\{(x,y,z):0\leq z\leq1,\ x^2+y^2\leq1\}\ \cup\ \{(x,y,z):1\leq z\leq2,\ x^2+y^2\leq2-z\}.
$$
The four panels show the three separate pieces and their union; there is no additional fourth component. In a meridional section, the boundary consists of the base segment, two vertical sides, and the parabolic cap. The outward normals point downward on the base, radially outward on the cylindrical side, and upward/outward on the cap.

\Image[paper-1-q1-closed-surface.png]
{title=Three boundary pieces and their closed union, shown as original surfaces of revolution}

= c
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

The <polynomial> <vector field> is smooth throughout the solid, so the <divergence theorem> applies. Differentiation gives
$$
\nabla\cdot\mathbf F=(2y+6x^5)+(-2y+4y^3)+1=6x^5+4y^3+1.
$$
The volume is invariant under both $x\mapsto-x$ and $y\mapsto-y$. Hence the <integrals> of the two odd terms vanish, and the <flux> is simply the volume. Using the circular cross-sections,
$$
\iint_S\mathbf F\cdot d\mathbf S=\pi\int_0^1 dz+\pi\int_1^2(2-z)\,dz=\boxed{\frac{3\pi}{2}}.
$$
The seams between the surface pieces have zero surface measure and contribute no extra term.

= 2
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= a
{parent=2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Set $y(x,t)=X(x)T(t)$ in the <wave equation>. A nonzero product obtained by <separation of variables> obeys
$$
\frac{X''}{X}=\frac{T''}{c^2T}=-k^2.
$$
The fixed-end <Dirichlet boundary conditions> require $X(0)=X(L)=0$. The zero and negative spatial <eigenvalue> cases produce only the trivial solution; the nontrivial <eigenfunctions> are
$$
X_n(x)=\sin\frac{n\pi x}{L},\qquad k_n=\frac{n\pi}{L},\qquad\omega_n=ck_n,\qquad n=1,2,\ldots.
$$
For each <normal mode>, $T_n''+\omega_n^2T_n=0$. By <superposition> and completeness of the <Fourier sine basis>, the general finite-energy solution has the expansion
$$
\boxed{y(x,t)=\sum_{n=1}^{\infty}\left[A_n\cos\omega_nt+B_n\sin\omega_nt\right]\sin\frac{n\pi x}{L}.}
$$
If $f(x)=y(x,0)$ and $g(x)=y_t(x,0)$, <orthogonality> gives $A_n=(2/L)\int_0^Lf(x)\sin(k_nx)\,dx$ and $B_n=2\int_0^Lg(x)\sin(k_nx)\,dx/(L\omega_n)$. Individual products are separable; their sum is the general solution.

= b
{parent=2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Assume the struck interval lies within the string, so $0\leq\ell-a/2<\ell+a/2\leq L$. The initially stationary straight string has $A_n=0$. Write its initial velocity as a <Fourier sine series> $g(x)=\sum_ng_n\sin(k_nx)$. Then
$$
g_n=\frac{2v}{L}\int_{\ell-a/2}^{\ell+a/2}\sin\frac{n\pi x}{L}\,dx=\frac{4v}{n\pi}\sin\frac{n\pi\ell}{L}\sin\frac{n\pi a}{2L}.
$$
Thus $y(x,t)=\sum_n(g_n/\omega_n)\sin(\omega_nt)\sin(k_nx)$. Immediately after the strike all the <energy> is <kinetic energy>:
$$
E=\frac\rho2\int_0^L g(x)^2\,dx=\frac{\rho av^2}{2}.
$$
The <orthogonality> <integral> $\int_0^L\sin(k_nx)\sin(k_mx)\,dx=(L/2)\delta_{nm}$ assigns <energy> $E_n=\rho Lg_n^2/4$ to mode $n$. Since the ideal modes evolve independently, their total kinetic-plus-elastic energies remain constant. Therefore the <modal energy of a localized velocity impulse on a string> gives
$$
\boxed{\frac{E_n}{E}=\frac{8L}{a\pi^2n^2}\sin^2\frac{n\pi\ell}{L}\sin^2\frac{n\pi a}{2L}.}
$$
The centre factor selects modes having significant motion at the impact location; the width factor accounts for cancellation across the finite hammer footprint. <Parseval's identity> ensures $\sum_{n\geq1}E_n/E=1$. The velocity discontinuities are understood in the mean-square sense and do not alter the <energy> <integrals>.

= c
{parent=2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

With the specified ratios, the two factors in the modal amplitude become $\sin(n\pi/5)$ and $\sin(n\pi/14)$. Hence a <normal mode> is unexcited precisely when one of these factors is zero:
$$
\boxed{n\in5\mathbb N\ \cup\ 14\mathbb N.}
$$
That includes all multiples of five or fourteen, with multiples of seventy counted once. The mode $n=0$ is not a fixed-end string mode.

= 3
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= a
{parent=3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Away from $x=\xi$, the homogeneous equation has solutions $e^x,e^{-x}$. A basis adapted to the two <boundary conditions> is $u(x)=\sinh x$ at the origin and $v(x)=e^{-x}$ at infinity. Their <Wronskian> is $uv'-u'v=-1$. The <Green function> must be continuous at $\xi$, while <integration> of $-G_{xx}+G=\delta(x-\xi)$ across the source gives
$$
G_x(\xi+,\xi)-G_x(\xi-,\xi)=-1.
$$
These conditions give the <Dirichlet half-line Green function for minus d2 plus 1>:
$$
\boxed{G(x,\xi)=\sinh(x_<)e^{-x_>}=\frac12\left(e^{-|x-\xi|}-e^{-(x+\xi)}\right),}
$$
where $x_<=\min(x,\xi)$ and $x_>=\max(x,\xi)$. The second term is the image that enforces $G(0,\xi)=0$; both terms decay as $x\to\infty$.

For the finite forcing interval, $y(x)=\int_0^\mu G(x,\xi)\,d\xi$. When $x\leq\mu$, split the <integral> at $\xi=x$:
$$
y(x)=e^{-x}\int_0^x\sinh\xi\,d\xi+\sinh x\int_x^\mu e^{-\xi}\,d\xi=1-e^{-x}-e^{-\mu}\sinh x.
$$
When $x\geq\mu$, the entire source interval lies to the left, giving the <Dirichlet half-line response to uniform interval forcing>
$$
\boxed{y(x)=\begin{cases}1-e^{-x}-e^{-\mu}\sinh x,&0\leq x\leq\mu,\\(\cosh\mu-1)e^{-x},&x\geq\mu.\end{cases}}
$$
This solution is unique: a homogeneous difference satisfying the origin condition is proportional to $\sinh x$, which cannot decay at infinity unless its coefficient is zero.

= b
{parent=3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

The left-hand value at the junction is
$$
y(\mu-)=1-e^{-\mu}-e^{-\mu}\sinh\mu=\frac12-e^{-\mu}+\frac12e^{-2\mu}.
$$
The right-hand value is
$$
y(\mu+)=(\cosh\mu-1)e^{-\mu}=\frac12+\frac12e^{-2\mu}-e^{-\mu}.
$$
Thus the piecewise <Green function> solution is <continuous>, with
$$
\boxed{y(\mu)=\frac12(1+e^{-2\mu}-2e^{-\mu})=\frac12(1-e^{-\mu})^2.}
$$
Its <derivative> is continuous as well: both one-sided values equal $-\tfrac12(1-e^{-\mu})^2$. Only the second <derivative> jumps, consistently with the step in the forcing and the absence of a <Dirac delta> source.

= 4
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= a
{parent=4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Use the <Fourier transform> convention
$$
\widetilde f(\omega)=\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}f(t)e^{-i\omega t}\,dt,\qquad f(t)=\frac1{2\pi}\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}\widetilde f(\omega)e^{i\omega t}\,d\omega.
$$
Since $\mathcal F[e^{i\Omega t}]=2\pi\delta(\omega-\Omega)$ in <distribution theory>, the carrier has transform
$$
\boxed{\widetilde c(\omega)=\pi[\delta(\omega-\Omega)+\delta(\omega+\Omega)].}
$$
The <modulation property of the Fourier transform> translates the message spectrum to the two carrier frequencies. Therefore
$$
\boxed{\widetilde\phi(\omega)=\pi[\delta(\omega-\Omega)+\delta(\omega+\Omega)]+\frac12[\widetilde s(\omega-\Omega)+\widetilde s(\omega+\Omega)].}
$$
The first term represents the unmodulated <carrier wave>; the other terms are the <amplitude modulation sidebands>.

= b
{parent=4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

A direct way to avoid the singular transform of $1/t^2$ is to invert a <triangular function>. Evenness gives
$$
\frac1{2\pi}\int_{-1}^1\pi(1-|\omega|)e^{i\omega t}\,d\omega=\int_0^1(1-\omega)\cos(\omega t)\,d\omega=\frac{1-\cos t}{t^2}.
$$
At $t=0$ the limit is $1/2$, so there is no singularity in the original signal. Thus
$$
\boxed{\widetilde s(\omega)=\pi(1-|\omega|)_+,\qquad (r)_+=\max(r,0).}
$$
Alternatively, the hint uses the <Hadamard finite-part reciprocal-power distribution>, whose <distributional Fourier transform> is $\mathcal F[\operatorname{fp}(1/t^2)]=-\pi|\omega|$; combining its three translates gives $\pi(|\omega-1|+|\omega+1|-2|\omega|)/2$, the same triangle. Treating $1/t^2$ as an ordinary integrable function would be invalid.

The message spectrum has height $\pi$ at zero, vanishing at $\omega=\pm1$. For $\Omega>1$, the modulated continuous spectrum consists of disjoint triangles of height $\pi/2$, centred at $\pm\Omega$, each with half-width one. In addition there is a <Dirac delta> carrier line of weight $\pi$ at each centre. These lines have specified area, not a finite height; arrows mark them schematically.

\Image[paper-1-q4-modulation-spectra.png]
{title=Triangular message spectrum, separated sidebands, and overlapping sidebands with the prescribed carrier-rejecting filter}

= c
{parent=4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

At $\Omega=3/4$, the two continuous triangles overlap near zero. Their amplitudes add, giving a central plateau. Explicitly, write $C(\omega)$ for the continuous part of $\widetilde\phi$:
$$
C(\omega)=\begin{cases}\pi/4,&|\omega|\leq1/4,\\\dfrac\pi2(1/4+|\omega|),&1/4<|\omega|\leq3/4,\\\dfrac\pi2(7/4-|\omega|),&3/4<|\omega|<7/4,\\0,&|\omega|\geq7/4.\end{cases}
$$
There are also the two carrier <Dirac delta> lines $\pi\delta(\omega\mp3/4)$. The last panel of the preceding figure shows this spectrum and the retained central portion. The prescribed strict cutoff removes the carriers and keeps
$$
\boxed{\widetilde\rho(\omega)=\begin{cases}\pi/4,&|\omega|\leq1/4,\\\dfrac\pi2(1/4+|\omega|),&1/4<|\omega|<3/4,\\0,&|\omega|\geq3/4.\end{cases}}
$$
The values of the ordinary function at individual endpoints do not affect its inverse transform. To handle the carrier lines, one must additionally assign zero filter gain to those lines, as the carrier-rejecting prescription indicates. A discontinuous mask has no canonical product with a <Dirac delta> at its discontinuity; a different physical boundary gain would leave a corresponding multiple of $\cos(3t/4)$.

The retained spectrum is real and even. Its inverse <Fourier transform> is
$$
\rho(t)=\frac14\int_0^{1/4}\cos(\omega t)\,d\omega+\frac12\int_{1/4}^{3/4}(1/4+\omega)\cos(\omega t)\,d\omega.
$$
Integrating the linear factors yields
$$
\boxed{\rho(t)=\frac{\sin(3t/4)}{2t}+\frac{\cos(3t/4)-\cos(t/4)}{2t^2},\qquad t\neq0.}
$$
The removable value is $\boxed{\rho(0)=1/4}$, obtained either by expansion or by integrating $\widetilde\rho/(2\pi)$. Because the carrier <frequency> is below the message bandwidth, this is <Fourier filtering of overlapping amplitude-modulation sidebands>; the usual picture of two isolated bands in <single-sideband modulation> does not apply without that qualification.

= 5
