= a
{parent=5}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

The inverse is assumed to exist. First,
$$
(I+M)(I-M)=I-M^2=(I-M)(I+M).
$$
By <matrix commutation with an inverse>, multiplying on both sides by $(I-M)^{-1}$ gives
$$
\boxed{(I+M)(I-M)^{-1}=(I-M)^{-1}(I+M).}
$$
An equivalent calculation writes $I+M=2I-(I-M)$, so both products equal $2(I-M)^{-1}-I$. No convergent infinite <matrix> series is needed.

= b
{parent=5}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Let $Q$ be the real <orthogonal matrix>. If $Qv=\lambda v$ for a nonzero complex <eigenvector>, then preservation of the complex Euclidean <norm> gives
$$
\|v\|^2=\|Qv\|^2=|\lambda|^2\|v\|^2,
$$
so $|\lambda|=1$. Its <characteristic polynomial> has real coefficients, hence nonreal <eigenvalues> occur in conjugate pairs. Such a pair is $e^{i\alpha},e^{-i\alpha}$, and the remaining real <eigenvalue> is $+1$ or $-1$.

If all three <eigenvalues> are real, each is $\pm1$; two have the same sign and can be paired as $e^{\pm i\alpha}$ with $\alpha=0$ or $\pi$. Thus in every case the spectrum has the form
$$
\boxed{\{e^{i\alpha},e^{-i\alpha},\varepsilon\},\quad\alpha\in\mathbb R,\quad\varepsilon=\pm1.}
$$
Their product shows that $\varepsilon=\det Q$.

= c
{parent=5}
{scope}

= i
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

First, $I-A$ is invertible. If $(I-A)x=0$ for a real <vector>, then $Ax=x$, whereas the <skew-symmetric matrix> identity gives $x^TAx=0$; hence $x^Tx=0$ and $x=0$. Thus its real <kernel> is trivial, which suffices for invertibility. The same argument applies to $I+A$.

Using $A^T=-A$ and the rule for the <transpose> of a product,
$$
N^T=[(I-A)^{-1}]^T(I+A)^T=(I+A)^{-1}(I-A).
$$
The factors $I+A$ and $I-A$ commute, as do the required inverses. Consequently
$$
N^TN=(I+A)^{-1}(I-A)(I+A)(I-A)^{-1}=I.
$$
Thus $\boxed{N\text{ is orthogonal}}$. This is the <Cayley transform of a real skew-symmetric matrix>.

= ii
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

For a possibly complex <eigenvector> $v\neq0$ with $Av=\lambda v$, invertibility of $I-A$ gives $(I-A)^{-1}v=(1-\lambda)^{-1}v$. Therefore
$$
\boxed{Nv=\frac{1+\lambda}{1-\lambda}v,\qquad\nu=\frac{1+\lambda}{1-\lambda}.}
$$
Thus every <eigenvector> of $A$ remains an <eigenvector> of $N$. For a real <skew-symmetric matrix>, its <eigenvalues> have the form $\lambda=it$ with $t$ real, so $\nu=e^{2i\arctan t}$ lies on the <unit circle>, consistently with <orthogonality>.

= iii
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

Taking <determinants> and using invariance under <transpose>,
$$
\det(I+A)=\det((I+A)^T)=\det(I-A),\qquad\boxed{\det N=1.}
$$
This <determinant> identity actually holds in every dimension. In dimension three, skew symmetry additionally gives
$$
\det A=\det A^T=\det(-A)=(-1)^3\det A=-\det A,
$$
so $\det A=0$. Hence there is a real nonzero <vector> $x$ in the <kernel>:
$$
\boxed{Ax=0,\qquad x\neq0.}
$$
The <eigenvalue> relation then gives $Nx=x$. Thus the <Cayley transform (Lie theory)> fixes an axis and is a proper three-dimensional rotation, including the identity as a degenerate case.

= 6
