= a
{parent=7}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

A complex function is <holomorphic> at a point if it is complex differentiable throughout some open neighbourhood of that point. Writing $\zeta=\xi+i\eta$ and $\psi=P+iQ$, a convenient sufficient and necessary criterion under continuously differentiable real components is the <Cauchy-Riemann equations>
$$
\boxed{P_\xi=Q_\eta,\qquad P_\eta=-Q_\xi.}
$$
They must hold throughout an open neighbourhood, rather than at an isolated point. Merely having partial <derivatives> that satisfy these equalities at one point is not enough without an appropriate differentiability hypothesis. For the composition $\psi=G\circ f$, <holomorphy> of $f$ and $G$ on the relevant open sets suffices; the additional condition $f'\neq0$ is needed when using a local inverse <conformal map>, not for <holomorphy> of the composition itself.

= b
{parent=7}
{scope}

= i
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Choose the branch of $\sqrt{(\zeta+1)(\zeta-1)}$ <holomorphic> in the <upper half-plane> whose boundary value at zero is $+i$. Then
$$
f'(\zeta)=\frac1{\sqrt{1-\zeta^2}},\qquad f(0)=0,
$$
and <integration> gives $\boxed{z=\arcsin\zeta,\quad\zeta=\sin z}$ on this branch. The normalization and intended upper-half-plane image fix the otherwise ambiguous square-root sign.

To see the image, put $z=x+iy$. For $-\pi/2<x<\pi/2$ and $y>0$,
$$
\operatorname{Im}\sin z=\cos x\sinh y>0.
$$
The bottom edge maps monotonically onto $[-1,1]$. The right wall maps by $\sin(\pi/2+iy)=\cosh y$ onto $[1,\infty)$, and the left by $\sin(-\pi/2+iy)=-\cosh y$ onto $(-\infty,-1]$. These boundaries describe the <conformal map of a vertical half-strip by sine>.

For completeness, the map is one-to-one in the interior: equality of two sine values forces arguments differing by $2\pi m$ or summing to $(2m+1)\pi$, and neither alternative yields two distinct points in this half-strip. It is onto the <upper half-plane> as well. Setting $w=e^{iz}$ gives $w^2-2i\zeta w-1=0$; the root product is $-1$, and no root lies on $|w|=1$ because that would make $\zeta$ real. Thus exactly one root has $|w|<1$. The identity $\operatorname{Im}\zeta=(\operatorname{Re}w)(|w|^{-2}-1)/2$ then gives $\operatorname{Re}w>0$, so $z=-i\log w$ belongs to the half-strip. The closure gives the stated boundary-inclusive region; <holomorphy> is understood in its open interior.

In this region the transformed function is $G(z)=\log(2+\sin z)$. Its only finite intrinsic <branch point> in the closure occurs where $2+\sin z=0$:
$$
\boxed{z_*=-\frac\pi2+i\operatorname{arcosh}2=-\frac\pi2+i\log(2+\sqrt3),\qquad\zeta_*=-2.}
$$
It lies on the left boundary. There are no singularities in the open half-strip. The points $\zeta=\pm1$, where $f'$ diverges, map to the bottom corners; $G$ is <holomorphic> through those corners since $2+\sin z\neq0$ there.

For the principal logarithm, the boundary segment $z=-\pi/2+iy$, $y\geq\operatorname{arcosh}2$, is the preimage of its cut $\zeta\leq-2$. Points on that cut above $z_*$ admit local <analytic continuation> after changing branch; $z_*$ itself is the genuine logarithmic <branch point>.

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Writing $\zeta=\xi+i\eta$,
$$
u-iv=\frac1{\zeta+2}=\frac{\xi+2-i\eta}{(\xi+2)^2+\eta^2}.
$$
Therefore
$$
\boxed{\mathbf u=\frac{(\xi+2,\eta)}{(\xi+2)^2+\eta^2},\qquad |\mathbf u|=\frac1{\sqrt{(\xi+2)^2+\eta^2}}.}
$$
The <vector field> points radially **away from $(-2,0)$**, with magnitude inversely proportional to distance from that point. It diverges on approach to that boundary point and tends to zero far away. This is the radial <potential flow> described by the logarithmic <complex potential of a two-dimensional potential flow>.

= iii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

The <chain rule> gives
$$
\boxed{U-iV=\frac{dG}{dz}=\frac{d\psi/d\zeta}{df/d\zeta}=\frac{\cos z}{2+\sin z}.}
$$
To make the directions explicit, set $z=x+iy$ and $D=(2+\sin x\cosh y)^2+\cos^2x\sinh^2y$. Multiplying by the <complex conjugate> of the denominator gives the <logarithmic flow in a sine-mapped half-strip>:
$$
\boxed{U=\frac{\cos x(2\cosh y+\sin x)}D,\qquad V=\frac{\sinh y(\cosh y+2\sin x)}D.}
$$
In the open strip $U>0$. The vertical component is negative near the lower left wall where $\cosh y+2\sin x<0$, and positive above that curve. On the bottom the flow is rightward. On the right wall it is upward, whereas on the left wall it points downward below $z_*$ and upward above $z_*$. The field is tangent to the boundaries except at the source and vanishes at the two bottom corners. At large $y$, $U-iV\to-i$, so it tends to a uniform upward field $(0,1)$.

Near $z_*$, $G'(z)\sim1/(z-z_*)$, showing a radial boundary source. The <streamlines> are the curves $\operatorname{Im}G=\arg(2+\sin z)=\text{constant}$; the figure shows their orientation without reusing the exam's graphics.

\Image[paper-1-q7-sine-map-flow.png]
{title=Radial flow in the upper half-plane and its image in a vertical half-strip}

= 8
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= a
{parent=8}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

For the normalized second-order equation, $x_0$ is an <ordinary point> if both coefficient functions $p$ and $q$ are <holomorphic> near $x_0$. If it is singular but $(x-x_0)p(x)$ and $(x-x_0)^2q(x)$ are <holomorphic> there, it is a <regular singular point>. Thus $p$ may have at most a simple pole and $q$ at most a double pole.

The distinction tells us which local series method is justified. At an <ordinary point>, two independent solutions are represented by ordinary <Taylor series>. At a <regular singular point>, the <Frobenius method> uses $(x-x_0)^r\sum_{n\geq0}a_n(x-x_0)^n$; the <indicial equation> determines the exponents, and logarithmic terms may appear in a second solution. Singularities worse than these are irregular and need not have this Frobenius form.

= b
{parent=8}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

The <Wronskian> is
$$
\boxed{W(f,g)(x)=\begin{vmatrix}f(x)&g(x)\\f'(x)&g'(x)\end{vmatrix}=f(x)g'(x)-f'(x)g(x).}
$$
Suppose constants $A,B$ satisfy $Af+Bg=0$ throughout the interval. Differentiating gives $Af'+Bg'=0$. At $x_0$, these form the homogeneous linear system
$$
\begin{pmatrix}f(x_0)&g(x_0)\\f'(x_0)&g'(x_0)\end{pmatrix}\begin{pmatrix}A\\B\end{pmatrix}=0.
$$
Its <determinant> is nonzero by hypothesis, so $A=B=0$. This is exactly <linear independence> of the two functions on the interval. No differential equation is needed for this implication; the converse for arbitrary differentiable functions would require additional hypotheses.

= c
{parent=8}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

The origin is an <ordinary point>. Substitute $y=\sum_{n\geq0}a_nx^n$ into the equation and collect the coefficient of $x^n$:
$$
(n+2)(n+1)a_{n+2}+(k^2-n^2)a_n=0.
$$
Hence the <recurrence relation> is
$$
\boxed{a_{n+2}=\frac{n^2-k^2}{(n+2)(n+1)}a_n,\qquad n\geq0.}
$$
There are two free initial coefficients. The independent even and odd <power series> begin
$$
\begin{aligned}
y_{\rm e}(x)&=1-\frac{k^2}{2!}x^2+\frac{k^2(k^2-4)}{4!}x^4-\frac{k^2(k^2-4)(k^2-16)}{6!}x^6+\cdots,\\
y_{\rm o}(x)&=x+\frac{1-k^2}{3!}x^3+\frac{(1-k^2)(9-k^2)}{5!}x^5+\cdots.
\end{aligned}
$$
The general solution is $\boxed{y=a_0y_{\rm e}+a_1y_{\rm o}}$. As a useful independent check, the substitution $x=\sin t$ reduces the equation to $d^2y/dt^2+k^2y=0$. Thus
$$
y_{\rm e}=\cos(k\arcsin x),\qquad y_{\rm o}=\begin{cases}\sin(k\arcsin x)/k,&k\neq0,\\\arcsin x,&k=0.\end{cases}
$$
For a nonterminating parity series, $|a_{n+2}/a_n|\to1$, so the <ratio test> in $x^2$ gives $\boxed{R=1}$. The <regular singular points> at $\pm1$ explain the natural obstruction.

There is an important exception to an unconditional radius-one answer. If $k^2=m^2$ for an integer $m\geq0$, the series of parity $m$ terminates at degree $m$ and is a multiple of a <Chebyshev polynomial>. Its <radius of convergence> is $\boxed{R=\infty}$; the other independent parity solution still has radius one. For $m=0$ the even solution is constant. Any nonzero combination containing the nonterminating solution has radius one. These are the full <power series of the Chebyshev differential equation>, including the terminating cases.

= 9
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= a
{parent=9}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Assume $c(y)>0$ is differentiable along the ray, with sufficient regularity for the following variation, and fix the endpoints. <Fermat principle> makes the travel time stationary:
$$
T[y]=\int\frac{\sqrt{1+y'^2}}{c(y)}\,dx.
$$
For the variational <integrand> $f=\sqrt{1+y'^2}/c$, the <Euler-Lagrange equation> is
$$
\frac{d}{dx}\left[\frac{y'}{c\sqrt{1+y'^2}}\right]+\frac{c'}{c^2}\sqrt{1+y'^2}=0.
$$
Expanding the <derivative> and multiplying by $c^2(1+y'^2)^{3/2}$ gives
$$
c y''-c'y'^2(1+y'^2)+c'(1+y'^2)^2=0,
$$
and therefore
$$
\boxed{c(y)y''+c'(y)(1+y'^2)=0.}
$$
Equivalently, the <Beltrami identity> gives $1/[c(y)\sqrt{1+y'^2}]=\text{constant}$. Mere continuity of $c$, as stated in the wording, does not by itself justify the appearance of $c'$; the displayed classical ray equation requires this additional differentiability.

= b
{parent=9}
{scope}

= i
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Introduce a constant <Lagrange multiplier> $\lambda$ for the fixed <arc length>. Up to an irrelevant constant term, the augmented <functional> is
$$
J[y]=\int_{-a}^a(y+\lambda)\sqrt{1+y'^2}\,dx.
$$
It has no explicit $x$ dependence, so the <Beltrami identity> gives
$$
(y+\lambda)\sqrt{1+y'^2}-\frac{(y+\lambda)y'^2}{\sqrt{1+y'^2}}=\frac{y+\lambda}{\sqrt{1+y'^2}}=k.
$$
For the hanging minimum choose $k>0$. Writing $y_0=-\lambda$, this becomes $1+y'^2=(y-y_0)^2/k^2$. Its nonconstant smooth solution is
$$
\boxed{y(x)-y_0=k\cosh\left(\frac{x-x_0}{k}\right).}
$$
One may verify it without dividing by $y'$ at the lowest point: the full <Euler-Lagrange equation> gives $(y+\lambda)y''=1+y'^2$, which the expression satisfies everywhere. The branch with negative $k$ is the inverted stationary chain rather than the hanging minimum. The <catenary minimizes the integral of height along a fixed-length curve>, so the positive branch is also the global minimum, not merely a stationary candidate.

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Equal endpoint heights imply
$$
\cosh\frac{a-x_0}{k}=\cosh\frac{a+x_0}{k},
$$
so $x_0=0$. The height condition then gives $y_0=b-k\cosh(a/k)$ and the hanging <catenary> is
$$
y(x)=b+k\left[\cosh(x/k)-\cosh(a/k)\right].
$$
Since $\sqrt{1+y'^2}=\cosh(x/k)$, its length fixes $k$ through
$$
2L=\int_{-a}^a\cosh(x/k)\,dx=2k\sinh(a/k),\qquad\boxed{L=k\sinh(a/k).}
$$
For the requested graphical argument, put $u=a/k>0$ and $q=L/a>1$. Intersections of $Y=\sinh u$ with $Y=qu$ determine the solution. Near zero the line has larger slope, while the hyperbolic sine eventually grows faster. Its slope $\cosh u$ increases strictly, so after turning upwards relative to the line it can cross it only once. The intersection at $u=0$ is excluded: it corresponds to infinite $k$ and the limiting length $L=a$.

\Image[paper-1-q9-catenary-uniqueness.png]
{title=Unique positive catenary parameter from a hyperbolic-sine intersection, with an example hanging chain}

More explicitly, $h(u)=\sinh u/u$ has <derivative> $(u\cosh u-\sinh u)/u^2>0$, because the numerator vanishes at zero and has <derivative> $u\sinh u>0$. Since $h(0+)=1$ and $h(u)\to\infty$, every $q>1$ yields **exactly one positive $u$, hence exactly one positive $k$**.

