= i
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

For an <eigenfunction> in the <operator domain>, $H\psi_n=E_n\psi_n$. Substitution into the <Rayleigh quotient> gives
$$
\boxed{F[\psi_n]=\frac{E_n\langle\psi_n,\psi_n\rangle}{\langle\psi_n,\psi_n\rangle}=E_n.}
$$
The cancellation only needs a finite nonzero <norm>; orthonormalization makes both denominator and <norm> equal to one.

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Assume $H$ is <self-adjoint> on its specified <operator domain>, and the admissible variations preserve that domain and any <boundary conditions>, so no boundary terms spoil self-adjointness. Let $N=\langle\phi,\phi\rangle>0$. The first variation of the <Rayleigh quotient> is
$$
\delta F=\frac{\langle\delta\phi,(H-F[\phi])\phi\rangle+\langle(H-F[\phi])\phi,\delta\phi\rangle}{N}=\frac{2\operatorname{Re}\langle\delta\phi,(H-F[\phi])\phi\rangle}{N}.
$$
At $\phi=\psi_n$, the residual vanishes, so $\boxed{\delta F[\psi_n]=0}$. More explicitly, for a normalized $\psi_n$ and $\phi=\psi_n+\eta$,
$$
F[\psi_n+\eta]-E_n=\frac{\langle\eta,(H-E_n)\eta\rangle}{1+2\operatorname{Re}\langle\psi_n,\eta\rangle+\|\eta\|^2}.
$$
For a fixed admissible direction scaled by a small parameter, the change is second order. This is stationarity, and is not necessarily a minimum for an excited state.

Conversely, vanishing first variation for every admissible complex direction yields the stationary equation
$$
\boxed{(H-F[\phi])\phi=0\quad\text{at a stationary }\phi.}
$$
At the original stationary point, this is $(H-F[\psi_n])\psi_n=0$. **The printed equation with an arbitrary perturbed $\phi$ in the final factor is not generally true.** Taking $\phi=\psi_n+\epsilon\psi_m$ with $E_m\neq E_n$ gives $(H-E_n)\phi=\epsilon(E_m-E_n)\psi_m\neq0$, even though the first-order change in $F$ is zero. Thus the valid claim is the stationary equation at the extremal state, not an exact <eigenvalue> equation for every variation.

= iii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

Assume a complete <orthonormal eigenbasis> and a <self-adjoint operator> bounded below, with the trial state in its <operator domain> or associated energy-form domain. The expansion and <orthogonality> give
$$
\langle\phi,\phi\rangle=\sum_n|a_n|^2,\qquad\langle\phi,H\phi\rangle=\sum_nE_n|a_n|^2.
$$
Consequently
$$
\boxed{F[\phi]=\frac{\sum_nE_n|a_n|^2}{\sum_n|a_n|^2}\geq E_0.}
$$
It is a weighted average of <eigenvalues>, all at least $E_0$. This is the <Rayleigh-Ritz variational principle>; equality holds precisely when the trial state lies in the <ground-state subspace>. A continuous part of the spectrum would require the corresponding spectral <integral> in place of part of the sum.

= b
{parent=10}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Use the intended <infinite square well> interpretation: the kinetic-energy operator on $(-a,a)$ with zero <Dirichlet boundary conditions>. The variational <energy> is most safely evaluated through its <quadratic form>,
$$
F[\phi]=\frac{\hbar^2}{2m}\frac{\int_{-a}^a|\phi'(x)|^2\,dx}{\int_{-a}^a|\phi(x)|^2\,dx}.
$$
The boundary term vanishes because the trial function vanishes at both walls. Its zero extension has <derivative> jumps there, so the form interpretation avoids assigning an unrestricted full-line second <derivative> to it.

Set $u=x/a$ and $b=\lambda a^2$. Apart from an irrelevant factor $a^2$, the trial function is $q_b(u)=(1-u^2)(1+bu^2)$. <Polynomial> <integration> gives
$$
\begin{aligned}
D(b)&=\int_{-1}^1q_b^2\,du=\frac{16}{315}(b^2+6b+21),\\
N(b)&=\int_{-1}^1(q_b')^2\,du=\frac8{105}(11b^2+14b+35).
\end{aligned}
$$
Hence the <polynomial Rayleigh-Ritz trial space for a one-dimensional box> has <energy>
$$
F(b)=\frac{\hbar^2}{ma^2}\frac{3(11b^2+14b+35)}{4(b^2+6b+21)}.
$$
The denominator is strictly positive, since $b^2+6b+21=(b+3)^2+12$. Differentiation gives
$$
\frac{ma^2}{\hbar^2}\frac{dF}{db}=\frac{3(13b^2+98b+21)}{(b^2+6b+21)^2}.
$$
The two stationary parameters are $b_\pm=(-49\pm4\sqrt{133})/13$. The smaller is a local maximum; the larger is a local minimum. Since the <energy> tends to $33\hbar^2/(4ma^2)$ as $|b|\to\infty$, the latter is the global minimum:
$$
\boxed{\lambda_* =\frac{-49+4\sqrt{133}}{13a^2}\simeq-\frac{0.220750}{a^2},\qquad E_{\rm upper}=\left(7-\frac{\sqrt{133}}2\right)\frac{\hbar^2}{ma^2}\simeq1.233718703\frac{\hbar^2}{ma^2}.}
$$
The upper stationary value is $(7+\sqrt{133}/2)\hbar^2/(ma^2)$. Exact roots give a slightly tighter bound than substituting the rounded hints.

**There are two qualifications to the printed operator and comparison <energy>.** For the box with <Dirichlet boundary conditions> and width $2a$, the actual <ground-state energy> is
$$
\boxed{E_0^{\rm box}=\frac{\pi^2\hbar^2}{8ma^2}\simeq1.233700550\frac{\hbar^2}{ma^2},}
$$
which lies just below the variational result. The coefficient $6/5$ printed as an exact <eigenvalue> is inconsistent with this box problem. Also, merely declaring the differential expression to be zero outside the interval does not impose hard-wall confinement or specify a self-adjoint domain. Read literally on a space admitting exterior-supported states, it has zero-energy states outside and does not have the claimed positive ground <energy>. The boxed variational result is the well-defined answer for the intended confined particle; a different full-line operator would need its domain and matching conditions supplied.
